# IP Address

Unique identifier assigned to a device.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## IPv4 Structure

32-bit address

```text
192.168.1.10
```

Binary:

```text
11000000.10101000.00000001.00001010
```

---

## IPv4 Classes

| Class | Range     |
| ----- | --------- |
| A     | 1-126     |
| B     | 128-191   |
| C     | 192-223   |
| D     | Multicast |
| E     | Reserved  |

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Private IP Ranges

| Range                         |
| ----------------------------- |
| 10.0.0.0 – 10.255.255.255     |
| 172.16.0.0 – 172.31.255.255   |
| 192.168.0.0 – 192.168.255.255 |
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Subnetting

Subnetting divides a network into smaller networks.

Benefits:

* Better management
* Improved security
* Reduced broadcast traffic
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## CIDR Notation

Example:

```text
192.168.1.0/24
```

Meaning:

```text
24 bits = Network
8 bits = Host
```
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Common Subnets

| CIDR | Subnet Mask     | Hosts |
| ---- | --------------- | ----- |
| /24  | 255.255.255.0   | 254   |
| /25  | 255.255.255.128 | 126   |
| /26  | 255.255.255.192 | 62    |
| /27  | 255.255.255.224 | 30    |
| /28  | 255.255.255.240 | 14    |
| /29  | 255.255.255.248 | 6     |
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NAT (Network Address Translation)
NAT allows multiple devices to share one public IP address.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## How NAT Works

```
  Private Network
   192.168.1.10
   192.168.1.20
   192.168.1.30
        ↓
    Router NAT
        ↓
    Public IP
    103.x.x.x
```

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Types of NAT

| Type               | Description         |
| ------------------ | ------------------- |
| Static NAT         | One-to-one mapping  |
| Dynamic NAT        | Pool of public IPs  |
| PAT (NAT Overload) | Many-to-one mapping |

Router differentiates sessions using port numbers.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Cybersecurity Relevance of NAT

Advantages:

* Hides internal network
* Conserves IPv4 addresses
* Basic anonymity

Limitations:

* Not a firewall
* Does not stop malware
* Does not prevent exploitation

